From 3f7e13074971293cc83d0b80cd552c2630a6e100 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:00:19 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GUIInfo: Player.Process() labels named as in the CoreELEC
 builds

Skins made for the CoreELEC builds that have them (avdvplus, and the cpm
builds it comes from) draw their Dolby Vision and HDR badges from
Player.Process(video.hdr.type), video.dovi.profile, video.dovi.el.type
and the like. Kodi has none of these, so on Hades those skins show
nothing there. Give them, with the same names and the same meaning of
their values, worked out from what Hades already knows of the video that
plays:

- video.hdr.type and video.source.hdr.type: SDR, HDR10, HDR10+, Dolby
  Vision or HLG HDR, of the stream (HDR10+ while the frames drawn pass on
  HDR10+ metadata of their own; a stream that Hades sends as Dolby Vision
  made of its HDR10+ metadata or measured from its HDR10 picture stays
  HDR10+ or HDR10);
- video.dovi.profile, level, version.major and version.minor,
  bl.signal.compatibility, has.config, rpu.present, el.present and
  bl.present: the stream's Dolby Vision configuration record ("0" for a
  stream without Dolby Vision of its own, as those builds give it);
- video.dovi.el.type: full (FEL), minimum (MEL) or none, where the
  enhancement layer is drawn (native output, or rebuilt as HDR10); empty
  elsewhere for a stream that has one;
- video.dovi.l1.min, avg and max, as .pq and .nits: L1 of the RPU of the
  picture that went out last, in native output.

Hades never rewrites the Dolby Vision of a stream, so the video.source.
labels, which there describe the file before such a conversion, say the
same as the others. What Hades does not know is not made up: such a label
is empty. Not given: the .raw HDR types (the two lineages number them
differently, so any number would be wrong for half of the skins), the
additional HDR type of a Dolby Vision stream with HDR10+, codec.string,
dual.track, meta.version and the audio object labels.

The window system publishes the stream with the rest of its Dolby Vision
information (hades.src.hdrtype and hades.src.dv, kept by
DVBRIDGE::VideoStreamOpened()); CoreELECLabels.h turns that into the
labels, and has a host test. The names and the meaning of their values
are those of the CoreELEC builds of avdvplus and cpm (an interface); no
code of theirs is used.
